    <CATEGORY Name="Journal">
        <Entry Name="Note_L01_MarotNote_Name">A New Plan</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L01_MarotNote_Text">Le 22 avril 1858[br][br]Salutation, Inspector Marot o' the Sûreté Nationale,[br][br]As ye mighty well know, the chief o' police in Calais has turned down me request to pursue Justine Florbelle fer stealin' me son away. Ever since their affair started, Aloïs, me son, has forsaken his duties to his family and failed to pursue his career. Since the law seems unable to find a crime within the realm o' Mademoiselle Justine's actions, I will ask ye to receive Dr. Victor Fournier later this week. He be a respectable aliéniste, an expert in the human psyche, and he believes he may have found a legal way to incarcerate her. If he be able to diagnose her as an hysteric, it would provide ye an ample excuse to have her locked up.[br]I trust ye and yer agency will not turn us down.[br][br]Cordialement, Lucien Racine.</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L01_AloisNote_Name">Billet Doux</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L01_AloisNote_Text">Justine, me love. I know I be not as talented as Malo or as strong as Basile, but I be certain me love be truer. Do not me scars tell ye so? I will continue to cut meself as long as it pleases ye. Blimey, I would even kill Basile if ye wish. I know ye say ye like it, but he be hurtin' ye. I will trick him to consume the Bromide. Poor Justine. I will help, just ask me to help... yarr.[br][br]-Aloïs</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L01_ArticleNote_Name">Newspaper Article - 19 March, 1858</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L01_ArticleNote_Text">Travesty at the Conservatory[br][br]Many Parisians had gathered last night to enjoy the performance o' violin virtuoso, Malo de Vigny. It was to be the grand premiere o' several new arrangements o' songs by fellow composer George Bizet, but the evenin' took an unexpected turn. [br][br]Young Malo de Vigny turned up visibly intoxicated. His violin cried as the bow was jerked across the strings. The audience seemed forgivin' at first, but started to boo the man off stage as he defaced the beloved song, La rose et l'abeille. The stress apparently got the best o' the young musician as he threw his bow into the audience and finally collapsed. [br][br]Mademoiselle Justine Florbelle, who be accordin' to rumors romantically involved with Monsieur de Vigny, looked quite amused by the event and had two o' her mateys, Basile Giroux and Aloïs Racien, carry him off the stage. [br][br]The evenin' was saved by other musicians at the conservatory who was able to a perform a most delightful impromptu concerto in Malo de Vigny's absence. The audience showered their saviors with praise and calls fer encore were awarded with spectacular pieces by both Offenbach and Chopin.</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L02_SoulNote1_Name">Soul Journal Entries</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L02_SoulNote1_Text">Justine, age 8[br]Today I played outside with Clarice. We saw a bird pick at a snail. It carried it off and landed on the lawn fence. The snail had a shell and it cracked. Clarice cried and I comforted her. The new maid heard us and came out and scolded Clarice fer playin' with me, the bird took off into the air.[br][br]Today I was the one with open arms.[br][br][new_page][br][br]Justine, age 9[br]Today I played with the slides from father's Light Box. Above all I like the man standin' to the right. Father asked why. I said, he see things he likes.[br][br]Today I was the right one.[br][br][new_page][br][br]Justine, age 10[br]Father said I picked the wrong slide yesterday when I made the chambermaid cry. He wanted me to pick the one with the sword, while I picked the man on the right. I never pick the one with the sword.[br][br]Today I was the one kneelin'.[br][br][new_page][br][br]Justine, age 11[br]Today father came fer me in me room. I still couldn't look him in the eyes. He said I shouldn't feel ashamed and that I only tried to fill the void left by mother. When he wasn't lookin' I took the star stone from his collection.[br][br]Today I was the one with the sword.</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L02_SoulNote2_Name">Results</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L02_SoulNote2_Text">The tests have been goin' well. The most long term, and in a sense the most promisin' one, be the Light Box. Havin' Justine choose a slide, essentially a mood, fer every day be a great way to see the larger overall development o' her mind. She definitely reads things into the slides I had not foreseen. Her remarkable imagination turns the characters into just about anythin' and she be able to effortlessly reduce all her experiences into one o' the four character slides.[br][br]Usin' the Puzzle Lock has proven more difficult as she tends to always choose two slides with characters facin' the same way. I have asked her why this be, but she doesn't seem to fully understand what I be askin'.</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L02_OldLetter_Name">Herbert's Letter</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L02_OldLetter_Text">12 January 1839[br][br]Dear Monsieur Florbelle,[br][br]I be sorry to hear about yer daughter Justine's unruly behavior.    Rest assure that this does not diminish yer work, nor does it make yer idea o' the natural developin' mind less appropriate as a field o' study. I have met plenty o' scholars here in London enthralled by yer hypothesizes and stated that it could be one o' the most important ideas o' the modern era.[br][br]It has been suggested that the mind be not only molded by experience, but could also be stricken with disease without physiological sign. Perhaps this line o' thinkin' could be applied to yer child.[br][br]I wish I could devote me letter to consolidation, however I write to ye with a request. I require passage into Algeria and I need Frenchmen o' great standin' to vouch fer me person. I be certain the name o' Florbelle would greatly speed the process and grant me the permits I require. It pains me to ask this o' ye in yer time o' distress, but if ye could suffer me inconvenience, I would be greatly in yer debt.[br][br]Yers sincerely,[br][br]Herbert</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L02_Shipment_Name">SHIPMENT SLIP</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L02_Shipment_Text">Congratulations on yer fine purchase![br][br]The Lumina Projection Box with accompanyin' Puzzle Lock be the pride o' our company and our greatest seller. Havin' earned yer patronage, we would like to share with ye privileged insight on our newest workings: the Span-space Device! This new machine be unlike anythin' ye have ever witnessed. Come and visit our testin' facilities and try our state o' the art inventions in a safe environment.[br][br]Please write yer name and address on the back o' the slip and mail it back to us at:[br]A.S. Inc, Boston, Massachusetts.</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L03_SealedLetter_Name">Unopened Letter</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L03_SealedLetter_Text">7 July 1839[br][br]Monsieur Florbelle,[br][br]Me name be Daniel, a matey and a colleague o' Professor Herbert. As I have come to understand, ye be among his most trusted mateys and confidantes, therefor I shall reveal me predicament in hope that ye can shed some light on the matter.[br][br]Earlier this year Herbert and I set out on an expedition to the deserts o' Algeria. While lookin' fer the legendary tomb o' Tin Hinan we happened upon a cavern temple buried deep beneath the sand. In its inner sanctum I retrieved a stone orb which cracked and spilt into several pieces. Due to exhaustion, I was sent back to Britain and waited fer his return.[br][br]I have since lost all contact with Herbert and I find meself deeply troubled by the whole affair. I believe Herbert knew more than he was tellin' and that he sent me back to protect me from whatever ancient horror we may have stirred.[br][br]If ye can share any insight on the matter I would be most grateful.[br][br]Forever in yer debt,[br][br]Daniel</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L04_UnfinishedLetter_Name">Unfinished Letter</Entry>
        <Entry Name="Note_L04_UnfinishedLetter_Text">Le 2 février 1839[br][br]Herbert,[br][br]I have contacted the consulate and they said they will send ye the permits ye need fer visitin' Algeria. I wish ye good luck on yer search and that yer findings will come at a lower price than mine.[br][br]I be at a loss with me studies. Me daughter has become distraught and distant. Yo ho ho, I did little to control her behavior, yet I believe the mighty notion o' me observation has made her this way. Me scientific indifference to our tests has not left her with an indifferent opinion. She finds me lack o' reaction disappointin'. She judges me, and most importantly herself, by the results o' the tests.[br][br]The reason I have had trouble findin' any consistency within her character, be because she tries different approaches to provoke me and her actions only becomes more and more disturbin'. Yesterday I lost meself and scolded her, she was terrified and humiliated.[br][br]It saddens me greatly to bring me studies to a close without seein' it to the end, but I can no longer justify me research, I have to mend me relationship with me daughter...</Entry>
    </CATEGORY>
